 unrecoverable divergence.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.engine-layer-enforcement","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["project.failure-disposition-contract"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.9,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'A store that CANNOT push is detected at the first write, not the last. Proven by driving a write against a deliberately-diverged clone and observing the refusal on write #1.'","'the store had healed by the time I looked ... So the condition was transient' — a transient condition and a permanent refusal have different dispositions.","ABSENCE: no explicit disposition word (fatal/permanent vs transient/retryable) attached to the first-write refusal arm."],"finding":"AC3 introduces a new write-refusal disposition (a diverged store is REFUSED at the first write), but the plan never states, with an explicit disposition word, what the caller surfaces on that refusal — whether it is a fatal/permanent condition requiring recovery (fsck-recover) versus a transient/retryable one. The incident itself shows the store CAN self-heal transiently, so the disposition boundary matters: a caller must know whether to retry or to stop and recover.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"feea4e803dcc95d2e","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance criteria: 'A store that CANNOT push is detected at the first write, not the last.'","norm_id":"n13023eb5e8c3e0bd","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A write hits a store that is momentarily diverged: if the refusal is surfaced as fatal, a caller stops and runs recovery unnecessarily; if it self-heals, a retryable disposition would have let the write land — the plan does not say which the refusal represents."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.25,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Clarify whether 'detected at the first write' is EXISTING behavior to verify or NEW behavior to design; if new, state the mechanism (e.g. a divergence check on the write path / lock acquisition) rather than framing it only as a proof-by-observation, since sync currently detects divergence lazily on reads and the incident's writes stranded silently before the fsck/lock refusal.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6533,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["AC: 'A store that CANNOT push is detected at the first write, not the last. Proven by driving a write against a deliberately-diverged clone and observing the refusal on write #1.'","Plan note: 'writes made BEFORE the refusal appeared to succeed locally and were stranded silently. Only the last two were refused. So \"loud on refusal\" is not the same as \"loud on divergence\".'","src/rebar/_store/sync.py:170-172 — divergence reconcile 'runs implicitly on reads, so it is the wrong place to make that call: fail toward REPORTING'; detection is best-effort, not per-write.","ABSENCE: the plan does not state whether write#1 detection is EXISTING behavior to prove or NEW behavior to design; if the latter, no design/mechanism is described."],"finding":"The acceptance criterion 'A store that CANNOT push is detected at the first write, not the last' is stated as a proof-target but the plan supplies no mechanism for how first-write detection is achieved; the current store detects unrelated-history divergence lazily via best-effort reconverge on reads (sync.py runs 'implicitly on reads'), and the incident's refusal was at fsck/lock time — so as written this AC may be unprovable against current behavior (the plan's own 'asymmetry' note says pre-refusal writes succeed and strand silently).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7bc3323728ce87e9","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance criteria — 'A store that CANNOT push is detected at the first write, not the last.'","norm_id":"n8aaad62b44687dbb","priority":0.1467,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Implementer drives write#1 against a diverged clone and finds it succeeds locally (no refusal), because detection is lazy/read-time — the AC cannot be satisfied without first building per-write detection the plan never scopes."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3333,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"no","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"yes","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"n